; ======================================================================
;  hw_id.pi3 - Raspberry Pi 3 HwId backend: WHAT THIS COMPUTER IS.
;
;  Forum 575. The shared core used to state one board's facts as literal
;  prose - the banner named a part, a serial port and an exception level -
;  so the second board printed three claims that were all false about
;  itself on the first line an operator ever reads. The cure is the rule
;  every other family here follows: the core names no chip and asks the
;  board. This file is this board's answer.
;
;  NOTHING HERE IS A CAPABILITY. A board with no GPIO is a real board and
;  #CAP_GPIO says so; a board with no IDENTITY is not a thing, so there
;  is no #CAP_ID and no gate.
;
;  INCLUDE ORDER: after RaspberryPi3/Lib/mmu.pi3 (MmuEl),
;  RaspberryPi3/Board/memmap.pi3 (MonImageLo, HwMonBytes) and
;  Anvil/Core/format.pbi (PutAddr), and before Anvil/Core/flow_cmd.pbi
;  and Anvil/Core/help.pbi, which are the two callers. House rule 10:
;  this file includes nothing itself.
; ======================================================================

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  The three names.
;
;  HwIdBoard() is the PRODUCT name a person would use for the computer in
;  front of them, not a part number - the operator reading the banner is
;  identifying the board on their bench. HwIdCpu() is the core, because
;  the sentence it lands in is about the processor. HwIdTarget() is the
;  word that follows -t on the compiler's command line, and it is the one
;  that has to be exactly right: it is a checkable claim about how the
;  image was produced, and somebody diagnosing a bad image goes where it
;  points.
;
;  "Raspberry Pi 3 Model B" and not "Raspberry Pi 3": the Model A+ and
;  the Model B+ are different boards with a different radio and, on the
;  B+, a different Ethernet part, and an operator who reads this line is
;  about to decide whether a driver applies to what is in front of them.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdBoard()
  ProcedureReturn "Raspberry Pi 3 Model B"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdCpu()
  ProcedureReturn "Cortex-A53"
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdTarget()
  ProcedureReturn "pi3"
EndProcedure

; PMFBOOT v2's compiler-owned target identity. Numeric because it is an
; admission ABI rather than display text; 2837 is the append-only BCM2837
; target code declared by the compiler and by Anvil/Core/pmfboot.pbi
; (#PMF_TARGET_BCM2837), and tools/pi3_slot.py refuses any other value in
; a slot header.
Procedure.i HwPmfTargetId()
  ProcedureReturn 2837
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdEl() - READ, never assumed.
;
;  The firmware normally hands a Pi 3 over at EL2 and the loader's branch
;  changes nothing, so this monitor arrives wherever the firmware put the
;  loader. "Normally" is not a contract - config.txt can ask for EL3 -
;  and a banner that asserted a level it had not read is the exact defect
;  forum 575 was about. MmuEl() decodes CurrentEL's bits 3:2.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdEl()
  ProcedureReturn MmuEl()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayConsole() - what an operator is reading this on.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayConsole()
  PrintN("The console is the PL011 on GPIO 14 and 15 at 115200 8N1, 3.3 V TTL -")
  PrintN("a serial adapter, not RS-232. The firmware must have routed the PL011")
  PrintN("away from the Bluetooth part for it to be there at all; on a stock card")
  PrintN("that is the disable-bt line in config.txt. Its baud divisor is computed")
  PrintN("from the clock the firmware reports rather than from a constant, because")
  PrintN("a console brought up at the wrong divisor prints plausible rubbish.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayImage() - the image contract, which is the half of `version`
;  that really is different between the boards. The A64 backend is the
;  SAME one, and saying so is the point: somebody who reads "pi3" and
;  goes looking for a second code generator will not find one.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayImage()
  PrintN("  The A64 backend is the same one the other 64-bit boards use - the A53")
  PrintN("  and the A72 are both ARMv8.0-A. What differs is the image contract:")
  PrintN("  this is A/B slot content, not a kernel8.img. An immutable loader on")
  PrintN("  the card verifies a slot, writes its trial state, copies it to")
  PrintN("  $200000 and branches there with a fifteen-second deadman already")
  PrintN("  armed, which this monitor releases by confirming the slot. The card's")
  PrintN("  kernel8.img is that loader and is never replaced by an update.")
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdImageBase() / HwIdImageLen() - forum 586, the content stamp.
;
;  Measured from the linker's own symbols by
;  RaspberryPi3/Board/memmap.pi3, so the digest the core computes covers
;  the bytes that are actually running. On this board that IS the file on
;  the card, byte for byte: the loader copies the slot payload without
;  relocating or padding it, so unlike a firmware-loaded image the digest
;  here CAN be compared against a host-side hash of the payload inside
;  the slot container. Saying which of the two a digest is remains worth
;  the sentence - see HwIdSayImageWhere().
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure.i HwIdImageBase()
  ProcedureReturn MonImageLo()
EndProcedure

Procedure.i HwIdImageLen()
  ProcedureReturn HwMonBytes()
EndProcedure

; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
;  HwIdSayImageWhere() - and what the digest is comparable to.
; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
Procedure HwIdSayImageWhere()
  PrintN("  That extent is the linker's own image symbols, so the digest covers")
  PrintN("  exactly the bytes the loader copied out of the slot - the payload")
  PrintN("  inside the slot container, after its 256-byte header. It will match a")
  PrintN("  host-side digest of that payload and it will NOT match a digest of the")
  PrintN("  whole slot file, which carries the header as well.")
EndProcedure
